# Metal-Organic Framework (MOF)-Based Drug/Cargo Delivery and Cancer Therapy

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-an emerging class of hybrid porous materials built from metal ions or clusters bridged by organic linkers-have attracted increasing attention in recent years. The superior properties of MOFs, such as well-defined pore aperture, tailorable composition and structure, tunable size, versatile functionality, high agent loading, and improved biocompatibility, make them promising candidates as drug delivery hosts. Furthermore, scientists have made remarkable achievements in the field of nanomedical applications of MOFs, owing to their facile synthesis on the nanoscale and alternative functionalization via inclusion and surface chemistry. A brief introduction to the applications of MOFs in controlled drug/cargo delivery and cancer therapy that have been reported in recent years is provided here.

Ming-Xue Wu and Ying-Wei Yang\*
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)—an emerging class of hybrid porous materials built from metal ions or clusters bridged by organic linkers—have attracted increasing attention in recent years. The superior properties of MOFs, such as well-defined pore aperture, tailorable composition and structure, tunable size, versatile functionality, high agent loading, and improved biocompatibility, make them promising candidates as drug delivery hosts. Furthermore, scientists have made remarkable achievements in the field of nanomedical applications of MOFs, owing to their facile synthesis on the nanoscale and alternative functionalization via inclusion and surface chemistry. A brief introduction to the applications of MOFs in controlled drug/ cargo delivery and cancer therapy that have been reported in recent years is provided here.
## 1. Introduction
Cancer is a threat to the health of human beings that causes millions of deaths annually. Chemotherapy is still the dominant treatment method. However, traditional direct administration of therapeutic drugs to patients is no longer applicable due to intrinsic limitations, including undesirable side effects, poor pharmacokinetics and poor biodistribution.[1] Thus, various efforts have been made toward the development of drug vehicles for controllable drug release that achieve reduced side effects and enhanced therapeutic efficacy.[2] Accordingly, during the last few decades, many different types of nanocarriers have been developed for this purpose, such as inorganic mesoporous silica, quantum dots, metal nanoparticles (NPs), and organic micelles, liposomes, and dendrimers.[3] However, all nanocarriers have their own limitations in bioapplications; liposomes, micelles, and dendrimers usually suffer from low loading capacities,[4] and inorganic porous materials have undesirable toxicity and unacceptable degradability.[5] An ideal drug-loading system should possess the following basic requirements: carriers should have a large loading capacity; carriers should be nanoscale in order to facilitate the release of drugs by intravenous administration;[6] carriers must be biocompatible, i.e., they should be nontoxic or have low toxicity and should be easily degraded by the body’s metabolism. Furthermore, the release of drugs in drug-loaded vehicles is expected to be effectively controlled. As a result, extensive efforts have been devoted to applying various controlled drug release systems in cancer chemotherapy.[7] More importantly, scientists are currently focusing on the development of multimodal treatment systems to satisfy an evergrowing demand for efficient therapeutic strategies.[8]
Hitherto, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been designed via diverse synthetic strategies[9] and studied for many applications,[10] including gas storage and separations,[11] catalysis,[12] sensing,[13] nonlinear optics,[14] an increasing number of potential biomedical applications,[9e,10a,15] and many others.[7d,9a,15b,16] Figure 1 summarizes the main synthetic methodologies of MOFs and their applications. In recent years, MOFs (where $"\mathrm{MOF}^{\prime\prime}$ encompasses all possible dimension scales) have received extensive attention in the development of drug delivery and cancer theranostic platforms, particularly MOFs in the nanoscale regime.[9a,d,15b,17] Compared with traditional drug carriers, mentioned above, MOFs possess the following obvious advantages:[7b,18] Firstly, their versatile structures provide MOFs with diverse morphologies, compositions, sizes, and chemical properties, which endow them with multifunctionalities and stimuli-responsive drug controlled release. Moreover, it is notable that the modification of such materials has no significant alteration of their desirable physicochemical properties, i.e., MOF-based materials still maintain controllable size, shape, and high uniformity.[19] Secondly, large surface areas and high porosities facilitate MOFs with high loading capacity. Thirdly, weak coordination bonds ensure MOFs are biodegradable. In short, these desirable properties enable MOFs to be promising platforms for drug delivery, clinical tumor therapy, and other disease therapies.
The scope of this review will highlight developments of MOFs in the fields of controllable drug release and efficacious treatment of cancer, including drug nanocarriers and cancer theranostic platforms composed of individual MOFs (Table 1), stimuli-responsive MOFs (Table 2), and multifunctional MOFs (Table 3), respectively. The essential demands of MOFs for bioapplications are also addressed. Finally, the prospects and challenges for MOFs have been extensively discussed. In contrast to other excellent reviews that discuss the bioapplications of
MOFs from a broad perspective, this review differs in that it will focus on highlighting the applications of MOFs, from individual nanocarriers to multifunctional cancer theranostic platforms, and provides an outlook for the development of cancer theranostic platforms for MOFs and their potential clinical applications.
## 2. Individual MOFs as Drug Nanocarriers
MOFs intrinsically possess large surface areas, highly ordered porosities, and well-defined structures, which endow these materials with the capability of loading and releasing different cargos, especially therapeutic agents. In 2006, Férey and co-workers first proposed that MOFs have potential in drug delivery, owing to their considerable loading capacities and controlled release behavior.[20] They built up two Crbased MOFs, denoted MIL-100 and MIL-101, from trimers of metal octahedra and di- or tricarboxylic acids, which exhibited remarkable IBU adsorption with $0.35\ \mathrm{g\g^{-1}}$ of IBU/dehydrated MIL-100 and $1.4\mathrm{~g~g^{-1}}$ of IBU/dehydrated MIL-101. The release kinetics of IBU were studied in simulated body fluid at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , indicating that a complete release of IBU from MIL-100 occurred after 3 days, and from MIL-101 after 6 days. Compared with a similar material, MCM-41, these two hybrid solids showed comparable IBU dosages and kinetics in simulated body fluid; especially MIL-101(Cr), which may be due to its larger cage sizes and stronger interactions with IBU. Notably, this work developed a new method of MOF-based drug delivery.
Taking into account the toxicity of chromium compounds, some low toxicity MOFs have been applied for the delivery of drug molecules. For instance, varieties of available Fe-based MOFs offer many possibilities to achieve an adequate controlled release of various pharmacological molecules.[9a,69] In 2008, the much less toxic iron analogue, MIL-53(Fe), composed of terephthalate anions and trans chains of Fe (III) octahedra, was
