Zn-based MOFs have also been reported as nanocarriers due to the low toxicity of zinc ion. For example, Wang et al. have reported a chiral Zn-based MOF constructed from $5,5^{\prime}5^{\prime\prime}$ -(1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6-triyl) tris (azanediyl) triisophthalate (TATAT) and ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ for the delivery of anticancer drug 5-fluorouracil (5-Fu).[22] Adsorption of 5-Fu was achieved by soaking the prepared MOF in 5-Fu containing methanol solutions, and the hydrogen bonding interactions between 5-Fu and the MOF ensured a loading capacity of $0.5\textrm{g g}^{-1}$ . Drug was released from the drug-loaded MOF by dialyzing the asprepared drug vehicle against phosphate buffered saline (PBS) solution $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ at room temperature, and the drug release process lasted for a week. Barea et al. have reported a series of isoreticular MOFs composed of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and some functionalized organic linkers 1,4-bis(1H-pyrazol-4-yl)-2-X-benzene $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDP}\_{X}$ ; $\mathrm{\DeltaX=H}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , OH).[23] Following this, two antitumor drugs, mitroxantrone and [Ru(p-cymene) $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (pta)] (RAPTA-C, pta $\begin{array}{r l}{=}&{{}1,3,5}\end{array}$ -triaza-7-phospaadamantane), were encapsulated in the porous Zn-based MOFs. An et al. reported that four types of adenine-based MOFs (bMOFs) were selected for delivery of etilefrine hydrochloride.[29] Among them, bMOF-1 and bMOF-100 were constructed from a zincadeninate secondary building unit (SBU) and biphenyl-4,4′-dicarboxylic acid (BPDC), and bMOF-4 and bMOF-102 were built from the same SBU with azobenzene-4, $4^{\prime}$ -dicarboxylic acid (Azo-BPDC). Drug-loading was accomplished by soaking the bMOFs in solutions of etilefrine hydrochloride in ethanol, after which the drug was released into simulated body fluid until completion. Results showed that the loaded drug was released over a long time ranging from 49 to 80 days, although an initial burst release was observed. Cao et al. also synthesized a waterstable $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based $\mathrm{MOF-Zn_{8}(O)_{2}(C D D B)_{6}(D M F)_{4}(H_{2}O)]}$ $\left(\mathrm{CDDB}=\right.$ 4,4′-(9-H carbazole-3,6-diyl) dibenzoic acid) and 5-Fu was adsorbed into the host MOF.[24] The drug delivery capacity of the synthesized MOF was around $53.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ with a delivery time of 3 days.
Usually, there are two methods for drug-loading: one method is a two-step encapsulation of drugs by immersing the prepared MOFs nanocarriers into drug-containing solutions; the other is a one-pot encapsulation of drugs. In addition to the two-step encapsulation of drugs mentioned above, Morsali and co-workers reported the first example of in situ synthesis of a drug-loaded MOF at room temperature.[25] IBU-loaded MOF $\left(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})\right)_{\mathrm{n}}$ was studied as a drug delivery system with loading capacity of $15\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ and a progressive release over 12 days.
Recently, a series of zirconium-based MOFs, especially UiO-66 (UiO for Oslo University),[70] have received increasing attentions.[71] These are composed of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ secondary building units (SBUs) and dicarboxylate bridging ligands. UiO-66 MOFs are desirable candidates for drug delivery because of their two octahedral $(\approx11{\mathring\mathrm{~A}})$ and tetrahedral $({\approx}8\mathrm{~\AA~})$ cages, nontoxicities, and excellent stabilities.[28] Serre et al. studied the rationality of a variety of biocompatible MOFs as nanocarriers combined with experimental and computational systematic exploration.[28b] Cubic UiO-66 solids were constructed from zirconium oxoclusters and terephthalate anions bearing different functional groups on the aromatic benzyl ring (UiO-66_X for ${\sf X}={\sf H}$ , Br, or $\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\prime}$ ). Caffeine encapsulation was performed by simple immersion, indicating that UiO-66 were promising carriers of caffeine. In another study, Lin et al. reported the first example of UiO MOFs for co-delivery of cisplatin and pooled siRNAs to enhance their therapeutic efficacy by overcoming drug resistance in ovarian cancer cells.[26] UiO MOF was prepared with $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ and aminotriphenyldicarboxylic acid (aminoTPDC) bridging ligands, in which a cisplatin prodrug and siRNA were sequentially loaded by encapsulating and coordinating to metal sites on the MOF surfaces (Figure 2a). Green fluorescence was clearly observed in cells incubated with siRNA/UiO-cis, but not in those treated with siRNA/UiO or UiO-cis, indicating that the developed siRNA/UiO-cis induce cell apoptosis (Figure 2b–d). All these results demonstrated that such a co-delivery method would enhance the chemotherapeutic efficacy of cisplatin in vitro (Figure 2e). Interestingly,
Ling et al. introduced a continuous flow microreactor technology for preparation of downsized UiO-66 NPs.[27] High surface-to-volume ratio of the continuous flow microreactor facilitated efficient heat transfer, leading to fast nucleation of UiO-66 NPs in the nanoscale, meanwhile the growth of UiO66 MOFs could be controlled by residence time. Furthermore, the obtained $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -functionalized nano-UiO-66-2 (nano-UiO$66{\cdot}2{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ) was used for loading 5-Fu, with a loading capacity of $27\mathrm{wt\%}$ . This work provided a facile strategy for the preparation of MOFs for bioapplications.
## 3. Stimuli-Responsive MOFs for Drug Delivery
Smart materials have aroused extensive attentions especially in the field of bioapplications.[72] Among them, stimuli-responsive MOFs have been appealing candidates for controllable drug release. Typically, stimuli-responsive MOFs can be divided into two categories: single-stimulus- and multiple-stimuliresponsive MOFs. In this part, we introduce a variety of stimuli-responsive MOFs, including single-stimulus-responsive
MOFs and multiple-stimuli-responsive MOFs, which are able to achieve regulated delivery of loaded drugs upon activation by diverse stimuli, such as $\mathsf{p H}$ , magnetic field, ions, temperature, light, and pressure.
## 3.1. Single-Stimulus-Responsive MOFs for Drug Delivery
## 3.1.1. pH-Responsive MOFs
Of all porous MOF nanocarriers triggered by external stimuli, pH-responsive MOFs are the most widely investigated, especially for cancer therapy, because of the acidic tumor microenvironment and the sensitivity of coordination bonds to external pH.[73] Until now, many investigations have involved the pHresponsive properties of MOFs for drug delivery and cancer treatment. For example, Huang et al. reported an Fe (bbi) MOF, constructed from 1,1′-(1,4-butanediyl) bis (imidazole) (bbi) and ferrous ions by a deposition method.[30] In situ encapsulation of DOX was achieved by simply adding DOX into the bbi solution (Figure 3a) and, as expected, the loaded DOX could be released upon lowering the pH, due to the sensitivity of the coordination bond to the acidic environment (Figure 3b). Thus, a layer of silica was coated on the surface of the MOF to prevent the rapid decomposition of the hosting material for effective control over the release of DOX. More importantly, folic acid was conjugated to the surface of the as-prepared nanocomposites for targeted drug delivery (Figure 3c). Consequently, the MOF-based drug vehicle showed pH-dependent progressive release behavior and good anticancer efficacy.
Another pH-sensitive nanocarrier, poly(acrylic acid) $@$ zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (PAA $@$ ZIF-8), was prepared by ionexchange between $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ and poly(acrylic acid sodium salt) (PAAS), followed by reacting with 2-methylimidazole (HMeIM) in methanol solution.[31] Notably, the obtained PAA $@$ ZIF-8 nanocarrier exhibited high DOX loading capability $(1.9\mathrm{~g~DOX}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{NPs})$ , which may be attributed to electrostatic interactions between the negatively charged PAA and positively charged DOX, as well as the coordination interaction of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -DOX.
Consequently, confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) analysis showed that this pH-sensitive drug release formulation showed a faster drug release in buffer solution of $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ value 5.5 than at a neutral pH of 7.4. Such pH-dependent drug nanocarriers were taken up by MCF-7 cells through endocytosis and were nearly nontoxic to live cells, suggesting their potential bioapplications.
Shi and co-workers also reported a pH-responsive drug carrier, UiO-66 MOF, which was applied for alendronate (AL) delivery owing to its natural drug anchorages of $\mathsf{Z r{-}O}$ clusters.[32] Herein, an ultrahigh dosage of AL, up to $1.06\mathrm{~g~AL~g^{-1}}$ UiO-66, was obtained due to strong complexation of $\mathsf{Z r\mathrm{-O-P}}$ bonds. Next, the release behavior of AL loaded nanocomposites was assessed at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in PBS buffer at $\mathrm{pH}=5.5/7.4$ ; the released amounts within $60\mathrm{~h~}$ were $59\%$ and $42.7\%$ , respectively. Such release behavior may be attributed to protonation of the AL at lower $\mathsf{p H}$ , which weakens the complexation of $\mathsf{Z r\mathrm{-}O\mathrm{-}P}$ bonds. Nevertheless, over a prolonged time the release rate was higher at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ than that at $\mathrm{pH}5.5$ ; a unique behavior that is attributed to the higher stability of UIO-66 under acidic conditions than under neutral and basic conditions.[70] In vitro cytotoxicity measurements showed that AL-UiO-66 exhibited higher anticancer efficacy than free AL after $^{48\mathrm{h}}$ of incubation, indicating that pH-responsive AL-UiO-66 improved antitumor efficiency and made UiO-66 a promising carrier for AL delivery.
Interestingly, a Gadolinium (III)-based MOF, Gd-pDBI $\begin{array}{l c l}{{(\mathrm{pDBI}}}&{{=}}&{{1,4}}\end{array}$ -bis(5-carboxy-1H- benzimidazole-2-yl)benzene), was proved to be a potential pH-responsive drug vehicle.[33] In this work, Gd-pDBI was transformed to MG-Gd-pDBI upon mechanical grinding and showed good aqueous dispersibility. It was then used for intravenous injection. The pre-synthesized MG-Gd-pDBI showed higher DOX loading capacity than Gd-pDBI due to its enhanced surface exposure towards DOX.
Moreover, about $44\%$ of drug was released from the nanocarrier at $\mathrm{\pH\5}$ within 5 days, whereas only $22\%$ of loaded DOX was released at $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ after 5 days, suggesting that the obtained MOF possessed pH-responsive drug release. Both in vitro and in vivo experiments demonstrated the practicality of the pre-synthesized MG-Gd-pDBI for DOX release and its desirable biocompatibility without side effects to other major organ functions.
Tsung et al. reported a pH-responsive ZIF-8 with uniform $70\ \mathrm{nm}$ NPs as a theranostic system.[34] Negatively charged small molecules such as fluorescein camptothecin (CPT) could be encapsulated into the ZIF-8 NPs by one-pot encapsulation. The performance of pH-responsive release was driven by the dissociation of the ZIF-8 framework at the acidic designated target. Herein, CPT-ZIF-8 treated cells showed increased cell death compared with free CPT treated cells. Similarly, Zou et al. reported a one-pot method for synthesis of ZIFs (ZIF-8 and ZIF-67) to encapsulate organic molecules (anticancer drug DOX and dye molecules, such as rhodamine B, methyl orange, and methylene blue).[35] Research results demonstrated that the developed one-pot process could be used to encapsulate target organic molecules into the same ZIF crystals. ZIF-8 crystals loaded with $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ of DOX were proved to be efficient nanocarriers for cancer therapy via pH-responsive release due to the instability of ZIF-8 under acidic conditions.
Recently, the Qian group described an interesting cationic nanocarrier, MOF ZJU-101 $(Z)\mathrm{{U}=\aleph^{1}}$ Zhejiang University), for delivery of diclofenac sodium, an anionic drug.[36] The cationic host material was composed of zirconium and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine$^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate (BPYDC) with high diclofenac sodium loading capacity of $0.546\ \mathrm{g\g^{-1}}$ . Diclofenac sodium was released faster in the inflamed tissues $\mathrm{(pH}=5.4$ ) than in normal tissues $(\mathrm{pH}=7.4)$ , because the ion exchange between anions in the PBS and the drug occurred more frequently at acidic conditions, which weaken the Coulombic interaction between the cationic ZJU-101 and anionic drug. Therefore, pH-sensitive diclofenac sodium $\textcircled{a}Z]$ U-101 was expected to be a promising anti-inflammatory drug vehicle.
More interestingly, they reported the first example of PCN$221\ \mathrm{\MOF}$ as an oral drug carrier.[37] PCN-221 was prepared from 5,10,15,20-tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP) and $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , and then the anticancer drug methotrexate (MTX) was encapsulated by immersing PCN-221 in MTX solution. Subsequently, the MTX-PCN-221 with loading capacity of
$0.40\mathrm{~g~g^{-1}}$ was immersed in PBS solution at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $\mathrm{\DeltapH}$ values of 7.4 and 2.0, respectively. Experimental results exhibited that $\approx40\%$ of MTX was released at $\mathrm{pH}=2.0$ (around the pH of stomach) after 3 days, whereas $100\%$ of MTX was released at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ (close to the intestinal $\mathrm{\pH}$ ). Accordingly, the pH-responsive MTX-PCN-221 was expected to be a promising oral drug carrier.
## 3.1.2. Magnetically-Responsive MOFs
Magnetically responsive systems allow for diversity in drug delivery due to their potential superiorities in magnetic separation, magnetic targeting, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and magnetic hyperthermia.[74] Among these, magnetically guided anticancer drug delivery is a unique strategy to concentrate drug-loaded therapeutic probes in tumor sites to improve therapeutic efficacy, since its first introduction by Watson et al. in the $1960\mathrm{s}.{\dot{[75]}}$ Candidate nanocarriers based on MOFs for such a delivery approach are usually core–shell NPs, for example $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was often used as a magnetic core with a MOF shell. For instance, Zhang et al. described a targeted drug vehicle based on a magnetic MOF, i.e., $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C u_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ (HKUST-1, BTC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate) nanocomposites.[38] Nimesulide (NIM), an anticancer drug, was loaded in the magnetic nanocarrier by soaking $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C u_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ in the NIM trichloromethane solution. Magnetic measurements were carried out, suggesting that $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C u_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ possessed desirable magnetic performance for targeted drug delivery and separation.
In 2014, Guan and co-workers reported a one-step in situ pyrolysis method for the fabrication of $\gamma{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ . In their work, $\gamma{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}@{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53$ (Al) demonstrated its potential for controllable magnetic separation and drug release.[39] As expected, the magnetic nanocomposites exhibited controlled release behavior in physiological saline at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; that is, the encapsulated IBU was completely released after 7 days in three stages: firstly, about $30\%$ of the drug was released rapidly at the first 3 hours; then, $50\%$ of drug was released during the next 2 days; finally, the residual $20\%$ of drug was released over 5 days. These results verified that the magnetic $\gamma_{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL-}53(\mathbb{A}$ l) material was feasible for drug delivery. Very recently, versatile magnetically responsive MOF-based biomedical carriers have been developed and are discussed in detail in 4.
## 3.1.3. Ion-Responsive MOFs
Ion-responsive MOFs have introduced a new pathway for drug delivery. In such drug carriers, strong electrostatic interactions between the drugs and the frameworks control the diffusion and release of drugs. Thus, the strong electrostatic interactions between ionic drugs and ionic frameworks have attracted particular interest because the release of ionic drugs is a chemical stimuli-responsive process taking place only through ion exchange. For instance, the Rosi group fabricated an anionic $\mathrm{Zn_{8}(a d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O\cdot2M e_{2}N H_{2}\cdot8D M F\cdot11H_{2}O}$ (bioMOF-1) by introducing biphenyldicarboxylic acid to the mixtures of adenine and zinc acetate dihydrate.[40] Such anionic bioMOF-1 was explored to test its capability to storage and release of procainamide HCl, a cationic antiarrythmia drug. Because of the electrostatic interaction between anions and cations, a loading dosage of $0.22\textrm{g g}^{-1}$ was achieved and the cations in the biological fluids were expected to trigger the release of procainamide HCl. Therefore, as a controlled experiment, drug-loaded bio-MOF-1 was placed in a PBS buffer $\mathrm{(pH=7.4)}$ and nanopure water, which verified that drug release could be triggered by buffer cations.
Yang and co-workers developed a cationic drug carrier, MOF74-Fe(III), through the oxidation of neutral MOF-74-Fe(II).[41] The cationic MOF exhibited around $15.9\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ of the loading capacity of ibuprofen anions (Figure 4a). Interestingly, the preparation process of MOF could yield hydroxide, resulting in the presence of two distinctive Ibu− anions in MOF channels, thus, two different release behaviors were observed. Sodium ibuprofen and the other coordinated free anions were released at first, merely because of diffusion or ion exchange between the drug-encapsulated MOF and PBS solution. The following release profile of the targeted drug was triggered by phosphate anions through competitive adsorption (Figure 4b).
## 3.1.4. Temperature-Responsive MOFs
Generally, temperature-responsive nanocarriers are materials that are sensitive to slight temperature changes at the physiological temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Poly(N-isopropyl acrylamide) (PNIPAM) is a promising building block for thermosensitive drug nanocarriers that possess a lower critical solution temperature.[76] When the temperature is lowered to below its cloud point ( $T_{\mathrm{c}},$ around $32~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ), PNIPAM exhibits hydrophilicity and tends to dissolve in water; however, it also forms an aggregate. Accordingly, Sada et al. demonstrated a switchable UiO-66- PNIPAM nanocarrier (Figure 5a).[42] Resorufin, caffeine, and procainamide were loaded in the nanocarrier by soaking the UiO-66-PNIPAM in guest solutions, then the release behaviors were assessed at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ or $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 5b). As expected, the amount of released drug increased at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and almost halted at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 5c), which indicated that controlled release was driven by temperature variation.
