## 3.1.5. Pressure-Responsive MOFs
To avoid the premature release of drugs until they reach at pathological tissues, above-mentioned responsive MOFs have been developed with prolonged release time and enhanced therapeutic effcacy. In addition to the above commonly mentioned stimuli, pressure has also been employed for controllable drug release. Recently, Qian and co-workers reported a $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOF builted from (2E,2E′)-3,3′-(2-fluoro-1,4-phenylene) diacrylic acid $\left(\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PDA}\right)$ and zirconium cluster with high model drug diclofenac sodium (DS) loading capacity of $58.80~\mathrm{wt\%}$ due to its enhanced polarity and extended organic spacer.[44] The DSrelease kinetics of such drug-loaded MOF could be adjusted through varying pressure, resulting in a prolonged release time between 2–8 days. Such formulation showed a new method for responsive MOF-based drug delivery.
## 3.2. Multiple-Stimuli-Responsive MOFs for Drug Delivery
The complexity of the human body environment limits abilities to precisely deliver drugs in the body using singlestimulus-responsive MOF drug carriers. However, multiplestimuli-responsive MOFs can be used as a better alternative to improve loading capacities and chemotherapeutic efficiencies. Pillararenes, one example of rapidly developed supramolecular hosts, have been widely investigated since their first discovery by Ogoshi et al. in 2008. This is due to their desirable properties, including their special structure, facile functionalization, and desirable host–guest performance.[77] Certainly, this new class of macrocycles is promoting the development of supramolecular chemistry and materials science.[78] Accordingly, many host–guest systems based on pillararenes have been developed,[79] in particular, pillararene-based nanovalves for drug delivery have recieved increasing attention in recent years.[80] It is conceivable that the combination of MOFs with pillararenes will reveal a new method for the bioapplications of MOFs.
Our research group has devoted much effort to design such versatile drug nanocarriers. According to the rapid development of gated materials,[81] multiple-stimuli-responsive drug delivery system (DDS) composed of MOFs and pillararene-based supramolecular nanovalves was developed for the first time by our research group, in collaboration with the Wang group.[45] UMCM-1- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was used as the host material to store cargoes of guest molecules, followed by modification of carboxylatopillar[5] arene (CP5) as a dual stimuli-responsive gatekeeper linked by positively-charged pyridinium (Py) stalks (CP5-capped UMCM1-NH–Py). In acidic tumor tissues or tumor cells, the gatekeeper of CP5 tended to be neutral, which weakened the noncovalent binding interactions between CP5 and the stalk, leading to unblocking of the MOF followed by the release of cargo. Upon addition of a competitive binding agent (methylviologen salts), which possess a higher binding affinity to CP5 than Py, CP5 was detached from the stalks, which caused the release of cargo. We integrated pH- and/or competitive binding agent-responsive strategies into a single drug nanocarrier (Figure 6), where the CP5-capped drug nanocarrier showed high encapsulation efficiency, negligible premature release, negligible cytotoxicity, desirable biodegradability, and biocompatibility. Further studies of the multiple-stimuli-sensitive nanocarriers based on MOFs with CP5 rings as gatekeeper have also been carried out in our group. For instance, we have reported another CP5-capped $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ 5-Fu nanocarrier.[46] In this work, UiO- $66\substack{\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ was modified with positively charged quaternary ammonium salt as stalks (Q) to thread the negatively charged CP5 rings, to form [2]pseudorotaxanes as stimuli-responsive supramolecular gates that regulate drug release. $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ can be a competitive binding agent to trigger the release of 5-Fu because of a higher binding affinity between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and CP5. In addition, increasing temperature could also weaken the noncovalent bonding interactions between the CP5 and stalks to stimulate the release of drugs. Therefore, the dual stimuli-responsive drug nanocarrier based on CP5-gated MOF outlines a new opportunity for therapy of central nervous system diseases.
Recently, our group developed a triply-responsive theranostic hybrid platform based on CP5-gated Zr-MOFs.[47] Positively charged quaternary ammonium salt stalks were tethered on $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ via post-synthetic modification, and then negatively charged CP5 was encircled on the stalks sitting on the surface of the MOF through host–guest complexation. As mentioned above, low pH, increasing temperature, and competitive binding agents could weaken the supramolecular interactions between the MOF scaffold and the gating nanovalves of CP5 to control the targeted release of drugs. Therefore, herein, a triple modal therapeutic trigger was integrated in one system (Figure 7). Controlled release experiments verified that the prepared therapeutic platform was promising for potential bone regeneration and bone cancer therapy because lower pH and higher concentration of ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ in osteoclasts and tumor cells could stimulate the release of the therapeutic agents via pH-responsiveness and $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ -competitive responsiveness. Furthermore, high temperatures could damage and kill cancer cells as well as weaken host–guest interactions to prompt gradually release of the therapeutic molecule (5-Fu). Furthermore, the triply-responsive drug nanocarrier possessed desirable properties, including negligible premature release, non-cytotoxicity, and good biocompatibility.
In addition, $\beta$ -cyclodextrin $({\beta}\mathrm{{\cdotCD})}$ is also one of the most promising supramolecular macrocycles that can be tethered onto the scaffold MOFs as gating entitles to regulate targeted drug release. For example, the Zhang group introduced a multifunctional MOF platform for drug delivery by one-pot post-synthetic surface modification of MIL-101 (Figure 8a).[48] After encapsulating DOX, MIL-101- ${\bf\cdot N}_{3}$ (Fe) was coated with $\beta$ -CD derivative $(\beta\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CD-SS-BCN})$ and a targeted peptide functionalized polymer, Lys (adamantane)-Arg-Gly-Asp-Ser-bi-poly(ethylene glycol) (PEG) 1900 ( $\mathrm{bi}=$ benzoic imine bond, K(ad)RGDS-PEG1900), could be tethered to the surface of the nanocarrier via a host–guest interaction. The proposed drug carrier possessed pH-responsiveness and redox-responsiveness due to the presence of a benzoic imine bond in K(ad)RGDS-PEG1900 and disulfide bonds upon linking ${\beta}\mathrm{{-CD}}$ on the MOF. Experimental results indicated that the suggested DDS possessed enhanced tumor cell uptake and that reducing agents triggered drug release, resulting in efficient drug delivery and cancer therapy (Figure 8b). Moreover, surface modification reduced the cytotoxicity of the loaded DOX on normal cells according to in vitro experiments, meanwhile in vivo experiments proved high chemotherapy efficacy of the DDS.
Recently, Wang and co-workers reported another dual stimuli-responsive MOF for on-command cargo release.[49]
Light-responsive release and competitive binding agent-responsive release were implemented in the system by tethering $\beta$ -CD on the surface of UiO-68-azobenzene ( $\beta$ -CD–capped UiO-68-azo) through supramolecular complexation (Figure 9a). Azobenzene groups provided photo-controllable cis/trans isomerizations and the isomerization of azobenzene from trans to cis promoted the dethreading of the gating ${\beta}\mathrm{{\cdotCD}}$ to regulate cargo release (Figure 9b). Furthermore, a competitive binding agent such as amantadine could also trigger the dissociation of the gatekeeper from the azobenzene stalks due to its higher binding affinity toward $\beta$ -CD (Figure 9c). Rhodamine B was loaded in the dual-stimuli responsive nanocarrier, indicating $\beta\mathrm{.}$ -CD–capped UiO-68-azo was stimuli responsive and without premature release. This work provided a promising platform for controllable drug delivery.
Some other attempts have been made to design multiresponsive MOFs carriers in addition to the above-mentioned supramolecular nanovalves systems. For instance, two biocompatible MOFs Fe-BTC and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC $(\mathrm{BTC}~=~1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylic acid) have been investigated for one-pot encapsulation of DOX at room temperature.[50] Drug release could be triggered in the presence of biocompatible liposomes and lower $\mathrm{\pH}$ , as carboxylate anions are protonated in acidic environments and break down the coordination between carboxylic acid group and metal center, leading to drug release by disruption of the MOF. Furthermore, stronger electrostatic interactions between DOX and liposomes also promoted the release of drugs. The obtained MOFs nanocarrier was initially used to release DOX in the presence of liposomes and low-acidity conditions, notably affording new opportunities in stimuli-responsive drug vehicles.
## 4. Multifunctional MOFs for Cancer Theranostics
Although numerous MOF-based therapeutic platforms have been widely developed in recent years, there are still many challenges that need to be addressed such as current treatment modality, which is still limited in traditional chemotherapy and whose therapeutic efficacy is hampered by multidrug resistance. Considerable effort has been devoted to the fabrication of multifunctional theranostic nanomedical platforms, such as versatile MOF-based diagnostic and/or therapeutic systems, to overcome aforementioned drawbacks to achieving superior anti-tumor efficacy, among which core–shell MOFs nanocomposites have shown marvelous potential as nanomedical platforms. In this section, we will mainly introduce core–shell structures of MOF nanocarriers, including polymer-coated MOFs, magnetic core– shell MOFs, and other core–shell MOF-based nano­composites. Furthermore, some multifunctional therapeutic nanoplatforms constructed from MOFs will also be described.
## 4.1. Polymer-Coated MOFs for Cancer Theranostics
Since the Lin group reported the first example of surface functionalization of a MOF material with a silica shell for the controlled release of metal constituents and luminescence sensing of dipicolinic acid,[82] an array of multifunctional core–shell MOF nanocomposites have been developed for biomedical applications due to their unique advantages, including enhanced stability, water dispersibility, biocompatibility, and tunable functionality. For instance, in 2008 the Lin group designed $\mathrm{Pt}$ -containing nanoscale coordination polymers (NCPs) constructed from $\mathrm{\bar{T}b}^{3+}$ ions and c,c,t-(diamminedichlorodisuccinato) Pt (IV) (DSCP), followed by a silica coating on the surface of the as-synthesized NCPs to enhance their biocompatibility and further functionality (Figure 10).[83] The controlled release behavior of silica-coated NCPs suggested that the nanocomposites could efficiently control the release of Pt species by varying the thickness of the silica shell. Silyl-derived c(RGDfK) was then grafted on the surface of the pre-synthesized nanocomposites to enhance cellular uptake. In vitro experiments exhibited high anticancer efficacies and good biocompatibility of the Ptbased NCPs.
Subsequently, they reported core–shell nanostructures of Mn MOF coated with a thin silica shell and functionalized with c(RGDfK) and rhodamine B.[84] Experimental results verified the target-specific uptake of the c(RGDfK)-modified Mn MOF by angiogenic cancer cells and their potential for MRI. $\mathrm{~In~}2009$ , they reported another theranostic platform based on iron-carboxylate MOF MIL-101.[51] In this work, optical imaging contrast agent, 1,3,5,7-tetramethyl-4,4- difluoro-8-bromomethyl-4-bora-3a,4a-diaza-s-indacene (Br-BODIPY), and ethoxysuccinato-cisplatin (ESCP) prodrug, c,c,t- $\left[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\right.$ $(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}$ (OEt) $(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H})$ ], were post-synthetically modified on the MOF. Because of the instability of the MIL-101 (Fe) in PBS buffer, a thin layer of silica was coated on the surface of NPs to slow down cargo release. Silyl-derived c(RGDfK) was subsequently functionalized onto the silica shell to enhance cellular uptake. As expected, the multifunctional MOF possessed appreciable imaging and anticancer efficacy.
Although the above-mentioned silica-coated MOFs possessed high drug-loading capacity, such nanoplatforms were limited by a burst release of drugs. Thus, the Lin group developed a new cisplatin-containing MOF composed of disuccinatocisplatin (DSCP) and $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , which was further stabilized by a lipid layer composed of cholesterol (chol), 1,2-dioleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphocholine (DOPC) and 1,2-distearoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphoethanolamine-N-PEG (DSPE–PEG2 K).[85] A targeting ligand anisamide (AA) that displayed an affinity for sigma receptors was functionalized on the surface of the nanocomposites to enhance cellular uptake. This formulation proved good anticancer efficacy and allowed for the preparation of other lipidcoated NCPs for nanomedical applications.
Afterwards, they reported another Mn-bisphosphonate MOF coated with lipid for theranostic applications.[52] The Mn-bisphosphonate MOF was constructed from zoledronic acid and $\mathrm{{MnCl}}_{2}$ in the presence of 1,2-dioleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphate sodium salt (DOPA), indicating high loadings of the zoledronate and considerable MRI property. Accordingly, the MOF was coated with a lipid/lipid–PEG layer to improve drug-release kinetics and biocompatibility, and further modified with a targeting group (anisamide) for specific cancer cell uptake. Such a theranostic nanoplatform demonstrated physiological stability, biocompatibility and imaging-guided cancer-target specificity, facilitating a huge potential application in the field of theranostic MOFs materials.
Horcajada and co-workers introduced an example of a specific modification of nanoscale MIL-100(Fe) with heparin to endow the MOF with appreciable biological properties via a one-pot method.[53] Compared with unmodified MIL-100(Fe), heparin-coated nanocomposites evidenced their colloidal stability and preserved their ability to accommodate high caffeine loadings with a more progressive release. More importantly, the heparin-coated MOF showed potential stealth properties that resulted from the influence of the coating on the cell toxicity and uptake, the activation of the complement pathway, the cytokines production, macrophage uptake, cytokine profile, and reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. Certainly, this surface modification method paved a new way to the design of desirable MOFs for theranostic applications.
Additionally, Tendeloo and co-workers were able to fabricate poly(ε-caprolactone) coated $Z\mathrm{r}$ based MOFs for anticancer drug carriers.[54] In this work, poly(ε-caprolactone)–tocopheryl poly(ethylene glycol)-succinate (PCL–TPGS) copolymer was engineered on the surface of UiO-66 and UiO-67 MOFs due to its biocompatibility and non-toxicity. Then, two model drugs, hydrophilic cisplatin and hydrophobic taxol, were used to investigate the drug-loading capacity and drug-release behavior of the drug-loaded nanocarriers. In vitro drug-release measurements indicated a sustained drug release and decreased burst effects. Moreover, the developed polymer-coated MOFs showed low toxicity as a result of the MTT assay using two cell lines (U-87 MG and HSC-3). All of these results suggested that such polymer-coated MOFs formulations were promising for further biomedical applications.
## 4.2. Magnetic Core–Shell MOFs for Cancer Theranostics
The employment of iron oxide as a core to fabricate MOF-based theranostic probes for cancer diagnosis and therapy have drawn considerable attentions because of their superior magnetic properties in, for example, MRI, magnetic hyperthermia, and magnetic separation. For instance, Fu and co-workers reported a theranostic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66$ core–shell nanocomposite that simultaneously performed drug delivery and MRI in vitro and in vivo.[55] The shell UiO-66 was used to encapsulate DOX and the strong superparamagnetic core $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ could be quickly collected under an external magnetic field and ensured its ability for MRI.
In addition, Ding and co-workers designed another $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ core–shell MOF $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathsf{Z I F}{\mathsf{-}}90)$ for accurate and visible drug delivery.[56] Considering the deep penetration capacity of magnetic field stimulus, an extremely low frequency alternating magnetic field (ELF-AMF) with a frequency of $20~\mathrm{{Hz}}$ was proved to be able to accelerate drug release from the magnetic ZIF-90 because of the magnetic thermal effect. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$
ZIF-90 drug carrier combined efficient diagnosis and chemotherapy in one system because the magnetic core provided MRI and magnetic thermal response simultaneously. Significantly, ZIF-90 was scaled down to $64~\mathrm{nm}$ and modified with rat serum albumin to improve their stability in a physiological environment and facilitated targeted delivery.
The Sahu group also developed multifunctional magnetic core–shell MOFs as theranostic nanoplatforms. They introduced isoreticular MOF (IRMOF-3) coated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs for paclitaxel delivery, followed by modification with fluorescent molecule rhodamine B isothiocyanate and folic acid.[57] Their research demonstrated that the integration of targeted drug delivery, MRI, cell imaging, and chemotherapy was realized in the multifunctional porous nanoplatform. Subsequently, a similar IRMOF-3 coated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocomposite was developed as targeted anticancer nanoplatform in their work.[86] Specially, a biodegradable $O$ -carboxymethyl chitosan (OCMC) was incorporated on the surface of a $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ core, instead of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to improve loading capacity and $\mathrm{\pH}$ sensitivity. More importantly, carbon dots (CDs) were encapsulated in the magnetic nanocomposites to realize optical imaging for the first time. The hybrid drug carrier exhibited high dosage of $\ensuremath{1.63\mathrm{~g}}$ DOX $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ magnetic MOF with pH responsive drug release, magnetic guided drug delivery, cancer cell targeting, optical imaging, and MRI. Effective theranostic efficacy, biocompatibility, and facile functionality made the magnetic MOF formulation a promising candidate for biomedical applications.
In 2015, Wang and co-workers reported multifunctional $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ poly(acrylic acid)/Au nanoclusters/zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) NPs $(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@P A A/A u N C s/Z I F{\cdot}8}$ NPs) for cancer diagnosis and visualized-synergistic therapy (Figure 11a).[58] The obtained nanomedical system possessed many therapeutic properties, such as facile magnetic separation, high DOX loading capacity, tri-modal imaging, dual pHresponsive drug release, and high in vivo tumor inhibition efficacy (Figure 11b). Tri-modal imaging of magnetic resonance, X-ray computed tomography, and optical imaging was utilized to combine the superiorities and circumvent the limitations of different imaging techniques. Thus, such multifunctional could produce reactive singlet oxygen $({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , and both of the magnetic core clusters, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{\Au}_{25}(\mathrm{Capt)_{18}}-$ could provide hyperthemal effects (Figure 13a). Additionally, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ presented magnetic targeting and MRI imaging capabilities (Figure 13b). More importantly, in vivo control experiments verified that the combined theranostic mechanisms (photo­ dynamic therapy/photothermal therapy/magnetism) suggested a higher inhibition effect than that of photodynamic therapy and/or photothermal therapy (Figure 13c), and the multifunctional $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{\cdot}\ensuremath{8{\cdot}A u_{25}}}$ nanocomposites showed high tumor inhibition efficacy without side effects on normal organs. All of these results confirmed superior therapeutic efficacy of the obtained theranostic nanoplatform, which combined a therapeutic mechanism and diagnostic capability in one system, making this system a good candidate for biomedical applications.
core–shell nanocarriers showed great potential for efficient theranostic applications.
## 4.3. Other Versatile Core–Shell MOFs Nanocomposites for Cancer Theranostics
Another interesting study for the multifunctional magnetic core–shell MOF theranostic platform was reported by Chen and co-workers.[59] Multifunctional $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C@ML}\mathrm{-}100(\mathrm{Ft}$ ) (FCM) nanocomposites were designed for simultaneous delivery of dihydroartemisinin (DHA) and Fe (III) as cancer theranostic nanoplatforms (Figure 12a). When Fe (III) reached the acidic environment of tumor tissue, it would be reduced to Fe (II) and then reacted with pH-responsive released DHA to yield ROS that induce cancer cell death. In addition, the magnetic core of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}_{:}$ with properties of magnetic guidance and MRI, was further used to encapsulate CDs for fluorescence optical imaging (FOI) (Figure 12b). In vitro and in vivo experiments exhibited dual-modal imaging-guided pH-responsive chemotherapy improved diagnostic and therapeutic efficacy for cancer treatment.
Great effort has been made to design multi­ functional core–shell MOFs as efficient theranostic nanoplatforms, in addition to above-mentioned magnetic core–shell MOFs theranostic systems, and many other versatile core–shell MOF nanocomposites have shown considerable potential for nanomedical applications.[87] For example, Wang and co-workers introduced a versatile $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\textcircled{a}$ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ag}$ nanocube platform for synergistic
Interestingly, Yang et al. fabricated a cooperative therapeutic system based on a multifunctional magnetic core–shell MOF.[60] The obtained $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{\cdot}\ensuremath{8{\cdot}A u_{25}}}$ nanocomposites integrated photodynamic/photothermal therapy, magnetic targeting, and MRI imaging into one theranostic system. Photodynamic effects were induced because the $\mathrm{\Au}_{25}(\mathrm{Capt)_{18}}^{-}$ clusters cancer treatment.[61] The inner core of the $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ MOF structure served as a $T_{1}\mathrm{-}T_{2}$ dual-modal MRI imaging agent and two-photon fluorescence (TPF) imaging agent; the outer shell of Ag NPs endowed the system with photothermal therapy capability and improved TPF imaging at the same time. DOX was loaded in the multifunctional nanoplatform with a high loading capacity of $600\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ , and drug release could be triggered by light due to surface plasmon resonance (SPR) of the noble ${\mathrm{Ag~NPs}}$ . Controlled experiments indicated that the combined therapeutic effect of chemotherapy and photothermal therapy was superior to sole chemotherapy or photothermal therapy. All of these results suggested that the $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[\mathrm{Co(CN)}_{6}]_{2}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ag}$ core–shell nanocomposites would be promising candidates for an imaging-guided cancer synergistic therapeutic platform.
Subsequently, the Chen group fabricated core–shell $\operatorname{PB}({\widehat{a}})$ MIL-100(Fe) dual MOF (d-MOFs) nanocomposites, integrating diagnosis and therapy into a single system (Figure 14a,b).[62] Multimodal imaging was introduced in the nanoplatform because the d-MOFs possessed $T_{1}/T_{2}$ and $T_{2}$ dual-modal MRI as well as FOI capabilities. More importantly, the inner Prussian Blue NPs were used for photothermal therapy in the NIR region. Furthermore, the as-prepared nanoplatform with high artemisinin loading capacity of $848.4\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was pH-responsive due to the degradation of outer MOFs in an acidic pathological environment. In vitro and in vivo experiments proved that the obtained core–shell PB@MIL-100(Fe) possessed high antitumor efficiency (Figure 14c,d) by combing multimodal imaging diagnosis, pH-responsive drug release, and chemophotothermal therapy in a single system. Similarly, Zhang and co-workers reported another $\mathrm{PB}@\mathrm{mSiO}_{2^{-}}$ –PEG multifunctional nanoplatform with high DOX loading capacity.[88] In vitro and in vivo experiments showed that such formulation has excellent antitumor capability by integrating MRI, photoacoustic imaging, pH-sensitive release, and photothermal-chemotherapy in one system.
Recently, Liu and co-workers reported a core–shell MOF for imaging-guided photothermal therapy; the first example of using MOFs as a contrast agent to guide photothermal therapy of cancer.[63] In their work, Mn-IR825 MOF was the core, which offered MRI and photothermal conversion properties in the obtained nanoplatform, and the outer coating of polydopamine (PDA) was used for facile modification of PEG to confer the nanocomposites desirable physiological stability. In vitro and in vivo experiments verified that the Mn-IR825 $@$ PDA–PEG nanoplatform possessed good biocompatibility and high photothermal therapy efficiency. Importantly, it could be degraded in vivo and showed rapid clearance via the renal excretion system. The developed work demonstrated great potential of such therapeutic formulation for cancer treatment in vivo.
Interestingly, they also reported a hafnium-based MOF built from ${\mathrm{Hf}}^{q+}$ and tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP).[64] This formulation integrated photodynamic therapy and radiation therapy in one system due to $\mathrm{Hf^{4+}}$ , which could intrinsically perform as a radio-sensitizer to improve radiation therapy, and the organic building block of TCPP could serve as photo­ sensitizer for photodynamic therapy. Experimental results exhibited that the developed nanoplatform possessed desirable biocompatibility owing to the outer coating of the PEG polymers and appreciable antitumor efficacy due to the combination of photodynamic and radiation therapy. Importantly, this multifunctional MOF-based nanoplatform showed no obvious side effects to normal organs and could be cleared away by the metabolic system of the modal mouse.
In addition, developments of core–shell MOF nanocomposites for feasible, accurate, and efficient diagnosis have also attracted increasing attention. For instance, Yang and co-workers reported a multifunctional MOF for dual-modal imaging of tumors in vitro and in vivo.[65] The inner core of the MOF was constructed from $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxyl ferrocene (Fc) and magnetic gadolinium (III) ions, and an outer shell of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was used for further conjugation of fluorescent dye RBITC and targeting ligand RGD, to provide their strong luminescence and targeting capabilities, respectively. The pre-synthesized multifunctional $\mathrm{Fc-Gd}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}(\mathrm{RBITC})\mathrm{-RGD}$ nanocomposites, with properties of low cytotoxicity, good water-dispersion, and high stability, enabled dual-modal $T_{1^{-}}$ and $T_{2}$ -weighted MRI in vivo, providing a versatile platform for targeting and imaging of diagnostic systems.
The Tang group introduced another core–shell $\operatorname{UCNP}\@$ Fe-MIL-101_NH2@PEG $@$ FAs for fluorescent/magnetic dualmode targeted imaging.[66] In the multifunctional nanocomposites, rare-earth-doped upconversion NPs (UCNPs) were used as the core to present upconversion luminescence (UCL) properties, and an outer shell of Fe-MIL- ${\cdot}101_{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF was used for MRI, followed by modifying with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -PEG-COOH to improve biocompatibility and stability. Subsequently, folic acid was modified on the surface of the as-synthesized nanocomposites for targeted imaging. In vitro and in vivo experiments verified that the tumor targeted dual-mode UCL/MR imaging nanoplatform possessed high sensitivity, low toxicity, and good biocompatibility. Their work opened new avenues to imagingguided cancer therapies by integrating the advantages of both UCNPs and MOFs in a single system.
## 4.4. Flexible MOF-Based Theranostic Nanoplatforms
Recent development of MOF preparation techniques have paved the way toward multitargeted nanomedical applications in both diagnosis and therapy, due to the facile integration of multifunctional components via inclusion and post-synthetic modifications of MOFs, together with the mature synthesis of MOFs. In addition to the aforementioned strategies, drug molecules as building blocks for MOFs are a straightforward approach to enable the development of future theranostic or multimodal NPs. For example, Dastidar and co-workers reported various $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ MOFs by incorporating nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and the organic linker 1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)ethylene (L) as part of the material for cell imaging and drug delivery.[67] Both of the synthesized MOFs showed potential for cell imaging and drug delivery because the imaging agent L and the drug molecules were part of the coordination framework. Moreover, good biocompatibility and biodegradability as well as comparable anti-inflammatory responses of the MOFs endowed such formulations with extensive potentials for theranostic bioapplications.
Recently, such formulations have also been studied as therapeutic systems, in which carboxyl-functionalized diiodosubstituted BODIPYs (I2-BDP) were incorporated into the UiO-66 MOF for photodynamic therapy via solvent-assisted ligand exchange (SALE).[68] The obtained UiO-PDT nanocrystals possessed low cytotoxicity in the dark with enhanced cellular uptake and photostability, together with high efficiency to generate ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for further photodynamic therapy of cancer treatment. All of these results demonstrate interesting strategies of designing functional MOF-based biomedical nanoplatforms.
## 5. Demands of MOFs for Bioapplications
Considering the demands of MOFs for bio- and related applications, properties such as the toxicological compatibility, biodegradability, and stability of MOFs need to be addressed. Firstly, MOF materials should be toxicologically compatible in terms of bioapplications, therefore it is essential that the MOF building blocks are non-toxic. Currently, Fe, Zn, Zr, Mn, Mg, and Cu are frequently applied for the construction of MOFs due to their appropriate toxicity determined by oral lethal dose 50 (LD50), i.e., $30\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ for Fe, $350~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ for $Z\mathrm{n}$ , $4.1\ \mathrm{g\kg^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Zr},$ $1.5~\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ for Mn, $8.1\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{\Delta\times\Delta}$ , and $25~\mathrm{g~kg^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{cu}$ , exhibiting their acceptable toxcity for bioapplications.[9e] As for exogenous organic building blocks, terephthalic acid ( $\mathrm{{.LD50=}}$ $5~\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ , trimesic acid $(\mathrm{LD50}=8.4~\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{kg^{-1}},$ ), 1-methylimidazole $(\mathrm{LD50}=1.13\mathrm{g}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ , and 2-methylimidazole $(\mathrm{LD50}=1.4\mathrm{g}\mathrm{kg}^{-1},$ ), are generally available for bioapplications. Furthermore, some endogenous MOFs based on endogenous organic linker have been obtained. Ideally, such formulations would reduce severe side effects.[29,40] In addition, some bioactive coatings are also acceptable solutions; for example, the above-mentioned MIL100(Fe) with heparin coating was endowed with enhanced biological properties such as lower immunoresponse, improved stability, and reduced cell recognition. To date, in vitro toxicological experiments have been conducted to assess the cytotoxicity of MOF-based nanocarriers determined by cell viability on various cell lines[89] Still, further efforts should be devoted to the evaluation of toxicity in vivo, not only to the observation of body and organ weights, but also to investigate metabolic pathways of such nanocarriers in vivo.[7a,69a,90]
For bioapplications, the biodegradability and stability of MOFs are other important concerns that need to be emphasized. The decomposition of MOF nanocarriers at a desired region is recommended to avoid endogenous accumulation. Much research on the stabilities of diverse MOF materials under simulated physiological conditions have been reported, for example, MIL-101 (Fe) possessed poor stability in phosphate buffer, while MIL-100 (Fe) is stable in water but decomposed after several days.[7a,51] Thus, the stability and degradation mechanism of various MOF carriers in vitro and in vivo need further systematic investigation.
## 6. Conclusion
In summary, we have reviewed recent significant progress made in adapting MOFs for drug delivery, especially for cancerous therapy and/or diagnosis. As outlined here, many remarkable MOF-based platforms have been developed for novel nanomedical applications, ranging from individual MOF systems, to functionalized MOF formulations, to current collaborative systems. However, there are still many challenges that need to be addressed for these systems before such nanocomposites can be applied clinically. Firstly, more effort should be made to fabricate versatile nontoxic or low-toxicity and good biocompatible MOFs to achieve prolonged blood circulation and to ensure that the decomposition products can be processed through the body’s metabolic system. Despite many excellent reports, research on the toxicity of MOFs and their biodegradation is still in its infancy. Future work may be focused on the fabrication of non-toxic MOF carriers by selecting endogenous building blocks or functionalizing MOFs with bioactive substances to reduce side effects. Additionally, the stability and degradation mechanism of MOF-based nanocarriers need systematic studies in vitro, and systematic in vivo investigations are essential to optimize the performance of MOFs before clinical applications. To emphasize an important point, it is not enough to investigate the therapeutic effect of nanocarriers and their effect on normal organs; further efforts need to be devoted to analyzing the metabolic mechanisms and metabolic pathways of nanocarriers in vivo. Importantly, subsequent long-term monitoring of organism is also indispensable. Furthermore, current studies of imaging and therapeutic applications are far from being applied clinically, and a comprehensive understanding of the mechanism of absorption–distribution–metabolism–excretion is needed. Finally, future endeavors should focus on the fabrication of multimodal MOF-based theranostic platforms combined with different mechanisms, for achieving excellent efficacy in anticancer or other diseases. Although theranostic MOFs for clinical applications are still a long-standing challenge in nanomedical development, scientists have made significant progress. It is expected that promising developments to MOFs for clinical applications will be made.
